## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a real-world KCA design for multi-tenant edge deployments, emphasizing PQC, attestation, isolation, revocation latency, and testing under churn.

## Key Concepts

- Edge attestation (TPM/TEE) and regional bootstrap
- Per-tenant SPIFFE IDs and cross-region trust anchors
- Post-quantum TLS (KEM/KEX) and crypto agility
- Short-lived certificates and revocation strategies
- Auditing and tenant churn tests

## Code Example

```javascript
// Verify SPIFFE ID and tenant binding from a signed certificate
function verifyCert(cert, tenant) {
  const spiffe = cert.subjectAlternativeNames?.find(a => a.type === 'URI' && a.value.startsWith('spiffe://'));
  if (!spiffe) return false;
  return spiffe.value.includes(`/tenant/${tenant}`) && cert.keyUsage?.includes('ServerAuth');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement tenant churn testing at scale?
- How would you validate cross-region trust federation under network partitions?

Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end KCA flow in a multi-tenant, confidential compute setting, emphasizing cross-region trust, ledgered revocation, and rapid shard rotation during rebalancing.

Key Concepts
- Hardware root attestation and regional bootstrap
- SPIFFE IDs and per-shard scoping
- Short-lived TLS certificates with TTL-based rotation
- Online/offline revocation and PQC crypto agility
- Tenant migrations and shard rebalancing stress testing

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: CSR enrollment and issuance flow
function enrollCSR(deviceInfo, region) {
  const attestation = regionalAttest(region, deviceInfo);
  const csr = createCSR(attestation, `spiffe://kca/${region}/tenantX/shardY`, 'graph-db');
  return kca.issueCert(csr, TTL=180);
}
```

